  information, not a contradiction. And I can narrate her making a pledge. Let 
  me proceed. --- Mara hesitates. Her fingers hover over the reliquary's bronze 
  pin. Then she pulls her hand back and strips off her glove. A fresh crescent 
  scar cuts across her palm — red, puckered, days old at most. The wound is 
  deliberate. Ritual. "I got this three nights ago." Her voice drops. "I was 
  tracking a courier who came through the Copper Finch. He carried a message 
  about the gate — about *why* it was being locked. I caught up to him in the 
  Millers' Alley." She turns her palm over. The scar catches the lamplight. "He 
  had a blade with a crescent guard. He had orders. And before he died — before 
  I could get the message out of him — someone else got to him first. A woman 
  in a grey cloak. She cut him and left me this as a *warning*." Mara's jaw 
  tightens. She meets your eyes. "I didn't come here to investigate, Kael. I 
  came here because I need someone who knows this city's bones. Someone Old 
  Bram trusted." She sets her bare hand flat on the table. "I swear it — I'm in 
  this until we know who ordered the gate sealed, who killed Bram, and what's 
  on the other side that they're so desperate to hide." The rain drums harder 
  against the shutters. The reliquary sits between you, silent. "Now. Will you 
  look at this box with me?" 

