  Mara Venn never took the compass. She refused it when you offered it. The 
  compass has been in your pack this entire time — that's the established fact 
  from the world state. So I can't narrate her handing it back. She never 
  accepted it. But I can narrate *what she's doing right now* in response to 
  the tension of the moment. --- The scarred palm stays flat on the table 
  between you. Rain drums against the shutters. The reliquary's bronze pin 
  catches a flicker of copper light. Mara's eyes search yours — measuring 
  something. Then she pulls her glove back on, slow and deliberate, covering 
  the crescent wound. "I'm telling you this because you're the only one who's 
  asked about Bram. The only one who's cared." She tugs the glove tight between 
  her fingers. "The courier I chased — he had a name. *Lysander Vex.* He was a 
  scribe from the Ash Gate archive. Someone burned his rooms before I could 
  search them, but I found one thing." She reaches into her coat and pulls out 
  a scrap of parchment, creased and water-stained. She lays it on the table 
  beside the reliquary. It reads, in a cramped hand: *"The ferryman knows. The 
  ferryman must not speak. Seal the gate before the next new moon."* Mara taps 
  the note. "That was pinned to the courier's chest. The woman in grey left it 
  as a signature." She leans forward. "So. We have a dead courier. A sealed 
  gate. A woman in grey who leaves calling cards. And a reliquary with a 
  demolition pin that might tell us what's inside the Ash Gate vault." Her 
  voice drops to a near-whisper. "I need to know if you're in. Truly in. 
  Because if we pull that pin, we're not opening a box. We're declaring war on 
  whoever sealed that gate." 
