  The coins clink onto the scarred oak between you. Two gold. They catch the 
  copper light and throw warm gleams across the water-stained note. Mara's eyes 
  drop to them. A pause. Then she scoops them off the table without ceremony, 
  pocketing them in her charcoal coat. "Fair." She nods once. "That makes this 
  official. You've bought my eyes and my blade until this gate business is 
  done." She taps the reliquary. The bronze pin shivers. "Now. Let's earn that 
  coin." She slides the iron box toward the center of the table. The demolition 
  pin is a thumb's-width cylinder of brass, set flush into the lid, with a 
  small ring at its head. "This is an archive vault key — the kind they use for 
  emergency access to the Ash Gate's inner seal. The pin arms a small charge 
  that cracks the lock plate. One pull, and we have... maybe thirty seconds 
  before it pops." She looks at you. "I don't know what's inside. Could be 
  records. Could be a trap. Could be nothing." She gestures. "Your call. Pull 
  it, or we find another way in." The rain drums. The lamp flickers. The note 
  on the table stares up at you: *The ferryman knows. The ferryman must not 
  speak.*... 
