[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The word drops like a stone into still water. *Now.* The reliquary's bronze 
  pin shivers once — a faint metallic *click-chuff* from deep within the iron 
  casing. Then a second sound: a low, building hum, like a trapped insect 
  trying to chew its way out. Mara's eyes snap wide. She shoves back from the 
  table, chair legs screeching against the floorboards. "Kael—!" The hum 
  becomes a whine. The iron box *bulges* at its seams — a hairline crack of 
  orange light splitting the lid — Then it *rips* open. Not an explosion. A 
  *detonation.* A flat, percussive *CRACK* that punches the air out of the 
  room. The reliquary shreds into a spray of iron fragments and splintered 
  bronze. The table buckles, one leg snapping as a chunk of iron embeds itself 
  in the wall two feet from Mara's head. Smoke. Acrid, metallic, stinging the 
  nostrils. The copper lamps swing wildly, casting shuddering shadows across 
  the room. Where the reliquary sat, there's nothing but a scorched crater in 
  the tabletop and a scatter of blackened metal shards cooling on the floor. 
  Silence. Then the rain again, washing against the shutters. Mara stands 
  frozen, one hand half-raised, her face pale in the lamplight. She stares at 
  the destroyed table. Then at you. "...You just destroyed the only way I had 
  to crack the Ash Gate's inner seal." Her voice is flat. Not angry. *Flat.* 
  "That charge was meant for the gate. Not for my lockbox." She exhales slowly. 
  Runs a gloved hand over her face. "Tell me you had a reason." 

