[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The name leaves your throat before you can stop it. "Old Bram." Silence. The 
  rain. The distant shouts. The copper lamp ticks. You didn't expect an answer. 
  You *knew* you wouldn't get one. But the words needed to be said — a marker 
  laid down, a name spoken into the dark so the world remembers he was here. 
  And in the quiet that follows, the memory rises unbidden. *The Ash Gate.* 
  You'd heard Bram speak of it only once, toward the end, when the drink had 
  loosened his tongue and the fear had tightened his eyes. He'd gripped your 
  arm with a ferryman's calloused hand and leaned close, breath sour with ale. 
  *"Don't go near it, boy. Don't ask about it. Some doors stay shut for a 
  reason, and the things that want 'em open… they don't knock."* He'd died 
  three days later. No wounds. No struggle. Just a cold body found near the 
  gate's iron seam. The fist pounds the door again. Wood groans. "LAST WARNING! 
  OPEN THIS DOOR!" The water-stained note lies on the scarred table. *The 
  ferryman knows. The ferryman must not speak. Seal the gate before the next 
  new moon.* You know how many days until the new moon. You've been counting 
  since you found the note. The obsidian key is cool and heavy in your palm. 
  What do you do? 

